---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import Ppi from '@/components/tools/calc2/Ppi.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="ppi-calculator">
  <Ppi client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the PPI calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      Pixels per inch is the number of pixels along one inch of the screen. The PPI calculator divides the
      diagonal in pixels by the diagonal in inches, then finds the pixel pitch, the physical size and the
      distance at which single pixels blend together.
    </p>
    <pre><code>diagonal in pixels = √(width² + height²)
PPI   = diagonal in pixels ÷ diagonal in inches
pitch = 25.4 mm ÷ PPI
blend distance = 1 ÷ (PPI × tan 1′)        (inches)</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A 27 inch monitor with 2560 × 1440 pixels has √(2560² + 1440²) = 2937.2 pixels along its diagonal, so
      2937.2 ÷ 27 = 108.8 PPI. Each pixel is 0.2335 mm wide and the screen is 23.5 × 13.2 in. Past about
      79 cm, normal eyesight cannot pick out single pixels.
    </p>

    <h2>Common screens</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Screen</th><th>Resolution</th><th>PPI</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>24 in monitor</td><td>1920 × 1080</td><td>91.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>27 in monitor</td><td>2560 × 1440</td><td>108.8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>27 in monitor</td><td>3840 × 2160</td><td>163.2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>55 in TV</td><td>3840 × 2160</td><td>80.1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6.1 in phone</td><td>1170 × 2532</td><td>457.2</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The diagonal on the box is rounded, often to the nearest half inch, so the PPI can be off by 1 to 2%.
      The calculation assumes square pixels, which almost every modern screen has.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
